Build the trees of different targets at the same time.

Adds the gbtree parameter `parallel_target_trees` (default false). With
`multi_strategy=one_output_per_tree`, a multi-target model with scalar leaves, such as
`multi:softprob`, builds one tree per target and iteration, one after another, each with every
thread. Trees of small data use many threads poorly, so the parameter builds the targets at the
same time instead, each on a worker with a share of the threads and its own OpenMP team, kept
between iterations. With the parameter, each target samples from its own seed, drawn in target
order, so the random draws do not depend on the thread count. Each target's tree is the one its
worker's share of the threads builds: XGBoost's histogram sums depend on the thread count, so the
model can differ in its last bits from one built with the targets in turn (with 9 threads and 3
classes, it is the model 3 threads build). Unsupported setups (GPU, external memory, other
updaters, `process_type=update`, distributed training, a single thread, `USE_THREADPOOL` builds)
fall back to the serial path, with a warning logged once per reason. winnowr sets the parameter
for fits with enough threads (see `builds_class_trees_at_once` in src/fitting.rs).

Source: nh13/xgboost commit 614391cde (branch nh_parallel-class-trees, on 293cad1a8; draft
https://github.com/nh13/xgboost/pull/2). The include/ and src/ hunks are carried verbatim,
including the test-only accessors (`TargetWorker*ForTest`) and comment changes, so the patch stays
the upstream commit's and can be dropped when it merges; its C++ tests, which use the accessors,
are not carried, as build.rs builds XGBoost's sources and not its tests. winnowr-xgboost's test
`parallel_target_trees_builds_each_class_tree_with_its_share_of_the_threads` checks the parameter.

Applied by build.rs to a copy of the vendored sources (paths are relative to the XGBoost root),
after patches 0001 and 0002.

